\section{Split independent Poisson formulation}
\label{sec:split_poisson}

The adiabatic split-Poisson calculation uses the same four scaled variables as
the OSC-style adiabatic system, but treats the gravitational potential
perturbation as an external inhomogeneous source during each mechanical solve.
For a mode of spherical degree $\ell$ and angular frequency $\omega$, with
$\sigma^2=\omega^2$, the code uses
\begin{align}
  y_1 &= \frac{\xi_r}{x^{\max(0,\ell-1)}} ,\\
  y_2 &= \frac{\sigma^2 \xi_h}
              {x^{\max(0,\ell-1)} S_2} ,\\
  y_3 &= \frac{\phi}{x^\ell S_2} ,\\
  y_4 &= \frac{\partial_x\phi}
              {x^{\max(0,\ell-1)} S_2} ,
\end{align}
where $x=r/R$, $S_2=q_x^3$, $S_3=P/\rho$, $S_4=\rho$,
$S_5=\Gamma_1$, and $S_6=A_{\rm osc}$.  The Eulerian pressure
perturbation is reconstructed as
\begin{equation}
  \frac{P'}{P} = x^\ell \frac{S_2}{S_3}\left(y_2-y_3\right).
\end{equation}

The mechanical unknowns are solved with the Cowling part of the OSC-scaled
operator, while the known Poisson contribution is moved to the right-hand side.
If $A_{\rm C}(\sigma^2)$ denotes the operator with $y_3=y_4=0$, and
$A_{\rm full}(\sigma^2)$ denotes the corresponding operator before the
Poisson rows are constrained, the split system can be written schematically as
\begin{equation}
  A_{\rm C}(\sigma^2)\,{\bf y}
  =
  {\bf b}_{\rm inv}
  -
  A_{\rm full}(\sigma^2)\,
  \begin{pmatrix}
  0 \\ 0 \\ y_3^\Phi \\ y_4^\Phi
  \end{pmatrix}.
  \label{eq:split_rhs}
\end{equation}
Here ${\bf b}_{\rm inv}$ is the inverse-iteration forcing term and
$(y_3^\Phi,y_4^\Phi)$ is obtained by solving Poisson's equation independently
from the current mechanical eigenfunction.  No factor proportional to
$1/\sigma^2$ is applied to this source term.

\subsection{Independent Poisson boundary problem}
\label{subsec:poisson_bvp}

The independent Poisson solve is performed in the same scaled variables
$(y_3,y_4)$.  The source is the current pair $(y_1,y_2)$, with $y_2$
constructed using the current value of $\sigma^2$.  The differential part of
the Poisson subsystem is
\begin{align}
  \frac{{\rm d}y_3}{{\rm d}x}
    &= c_{33} y_3 + \frac{1}{x} y_4, \\
  \frac{{\rm d}y_4}{{\rm d}x}
    &= -c_{21}\frac{S_4}{S_2}y_1
       + c_{42} y_2
       + \left[\frac{\ell(\ell+1)}{x}-c_{42}\right]y_3
       + \left(c_{33}-\frac{1}{x}\right)y_4 ,
       \label{eq:poisson_bvp}
\end{align}
with
\begin{align}
  c_{33} &= \frac{3-S_4/S_2-\ell}{x}, &
  c_{21} &= x S_6, &
  c_{42} &= \frac{xS_4}{S_3S_5}.
\end{align}
Regularity at the centre and the vacuum exterior condition at the surface are
imposed through
\begin{align}
  y_4 &= \ell y_3 \qquad (x=0), \\
  y_4 + (\ell+1)y_3
      &= -\frac{S_4}{S_2}y_1 \qquad (x=1).
\end{align}
The interior is discretised with the same alternating Simpson/trapezoidal block
formula as the OSC-style first-order system.  The first and last mesh points
use one-sided forms of the $y_3$ differential equation to close the
tridiagonal two-variable boundary-value problem.

After the independent Poisson solve, the code stores
\begin{align}
  \phi &= x^\ell S_2 y_3^\Phi,\\
  \partial_x\phi &= x^{\max(0,\ell-1)} S_2 y_4^\Phi,
\end{align}
and injects the corresponding physical source into the mechanical equations:
\begin{align}
  b_r &\leftarrow b_r - \partial_x\phi,\\
  b_h &\leftarrow b_h + \phi.
\end{align}
This source form is equivalent to adding the perturbed gravitational
acceleration to the radial equation and the perturbed potential to the
horizontal equation, but it is evaluated explicitly from the previous
eigenfunction iterate.

\section{Frequency iteration}
\label{sec:split_algorithm}

Each mode is computed by a fixed-point inverse iteration in which the Poisson
source is refreshed from the current eigenfunction.  The grid is progressively
refined with strides $8,4,2,1$.  At a given grid stage, the iteration is:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Interpolate the previous eigenfunction to the current grid, or build a
        regular seed eigenfunction if no previous iterate is available.
  \item Normalise the current shape using the largest absolute value among
        $\xi_r$, $\xi_h$, and $P'/P$.
  \item Solve the independent Poisson boundary-value problem
        (Eq.~\ref{eq:poisson_bvp}) from the current mechanical shape.
  \item Search in frequency by solving Eq.~\ref{eq:split_rhs} at trial
        frequencies in a local interval around the current $\omega$.  The
        trial frequency that maximises the response norm is retained.
  \item Update $\sigma^2$ with a relaxed step.  The step is limited to two per
        cent of the current $\sigma^2$ and is clipped to the asymptotic branch
        bounds.
  \item Solve the split mechanical system at the updated frequency, unpack the
        physical eigenfunction, renormalise it, and recompute the Poisson
        source for the next iteration.
\end{enumerate}
The calculation stops when the frequency change falls below the prescribed
tolerance on the final grid, or when the maximum number of inverse iterations
is reached.

\section{Comparison with OSC}
\label{sec:osc_comparison}

The split independent-Poisson implementation was compared with OSC frequencies
computed on the same OSC binary model.  The SISMO run used the OSC frequency
list as the starting mode list and then solved the split-Poisson problem
independently.  The requested interval was $n=-1,\ldots,-100$ for
$\ell=1,2$.  The available OSC reference file did not contain unique negative
radial-order labels over this complete interval: for $\ell=1$ it provided
42 labelled modes in the requested interval, and for $\ell=2$ it provided
97 labelled modes.  The quoted comparison uses the nearest OSC frequency with
the same $(\ell,m,k)$ and reports
\begin{equation}
  \left|\delta_{\rm rel}\right|
    =
  \left|
  \frac{\omega_{\rm split}-\omega_{\rm OSC}}{\omega_{\rm OSC}}
  \right|.
\end{equation}

\begin{table}
\caption{Nearest-match comparison between the split independent-Poisson
calculation and OSC for the requested interval $1\le |n|\le100$.}
\label{tab:osc_summary}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ccccccc}
\toprule
$\ell$ & $N_{\rm OSC}$ & conv. & mean & median & p90 & max \\
       &               &       & \multicolumn{4}{c}{$|\delta_{\rm rel}|$} \\
\midrule
1 & 42 & 39/42 & $4.868\times10^{-3}$ & $2.406\times10^{-3}$ &
    $1.155\times10^{-2}$ & $3.958\times10^{-2}$ \\
2 & 97 & 92/97 & $3.103\times10^{-3}$ & $1.637\times10^{-3}$ &
    $6.057\times10^{-3}$ & $3.680\times10^{-2}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}
\caption{Binned nearest-match frequency differences.  Empty bins indicate
that no uniquely labelled OSC modes were available in that interval.}
\label{tab:osc_bins}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
\toprule
$\ell$ & $|n|$ interval & $N$ & mean $|\delta_{\rm rel}|$ &
max $|\delta_{\rm rel}|$ \\
\midrule
1 & 1--5   & 5  & $1.543\times10^{-2}$ & $3.958\times10^{-2}$ \\
1 & 6--10  & 5  & $1.617\times10^{-3}$ & $4.101\times10^{-3}$ \\
1 & 11--20 & 10 & $1.829\times10^{-3}$ & $8.852\times10^{-3}$ \\
1 & 21--40 & 20 & $4.756\times10^{-3}$ & $1.283\times10^{-2}$ \\
1 & 41--70 & 2  & $2.909\times10^{-3}$ & $4.165\times10^{-3}$ \\
2 & 1--5   & 5  & $1.974\times10^{-2}$ & $3.680\times10^{-2}$ \\
2 & 6--10  & 5  & $3.437\times10^{-3}$ & $8.482\times10^{-3}$ \\
2 & 11--20 & 10 & $1.391\times10^{-3}$ & $6.555\times10^{-3}$ \\
2 & 21--40 & 20 & $2.311\times10^{-3}$ & $6.235\times10^{-3}$ \\
2 & 41--70 & 30 & $2.341\times10^{-3}$ & $6.695\times10^{-3}$ \\
2 & 71--100 & 27 & $2.026\times10^{-3}$ & $5.332\times10^{-3}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The largest individual offsets in the requested interval occur at low radial
order: for $\ell=1$, the largest nearest-match difference is obtained for
$n=-1$, with $\omega_{\rm split}=1.937629430$ and
$\omega_{\rm OSC}=1.863849858$, giving
$|\delta_{\rm rel}|=3.958\times10^{-2}$.  For $\ell=2$, the largest offset is
obtained for $n=-2$, with $\omega_{\rm split}=3.013650360$ and
$\omega_{\rm OSC}=2.906683423$, giving
$|\delta_{\rm rel}|=3.680\times10^{-2}$.  At higher radial order the typical
offset is smaller, but remains at the $10^{-3}$ level for the split
independent-Poisson algorithm.

